#### Q4: What is AAA Pattern?

**Theory:**
AAA stands for **Arrange-Act-Assert**. It's a template for structuring test cases to make them clear and organized.

**The Pattern:**

```
ARRANGE (Setup - ~50% of code)
├─ Create test objects
├─ Initialize test data
├─ Set up preconditions
└─ Prepare known state

ACT (Execute - ~10% of code)
├─ Perform the action being tested
├─ Call the function/feature
├─ Trigger the behavior
└─ Should be small!

ASSERT (Verify - ~40% of code)
├─ Check actual vs expected
├─ Verify the result
├─ Confirm all details
└─ Test fails if wrong
```

**Why This Matters:**

```
Without AAA (Hard to Read):
────────────────────────────
User user = new User("John");
user.setAge(25);
List<User> users = new ArrayList<>();
users.add(user);
int count = users.size();
if (count == 1) System.out.println("Pass");
else System.out.println("Fail");
[Very hard to understand what's being tested]


With AAA (Clear):
─────────────────
// ARRANGE
User user = new User("John");
user.setAge(25);

// ACT
List<User> users = new ArrayList<>();
users.add(user);

// ASSERT
assertEquals(1, users.size());
[Immediately clear: Setup, do action, verify]
```

**Example:**

```
Feature: Shopping Cart - Add Item

Test Case Using AAA:

// ARRANGE - Setup
Product laptop = new Product("Dell XPS", 999.99);
ShoppingCart cart = new ShoppingCart();
int expectedTotal = 1;

// ACT - Execute
cart.addItem(laptop);

// ASSERT - Verify
assertEquals(expectedTotal, cart.getItemCount());
assertEquals(999.99, cart.getTotal());
assertTrue(cart.containsItem(laptop));

Result: ✓ PASS
```

