### INTERMEDIATE LEVEL

#### Q6: What is Test Independence? Why Does It Matter?

**Theory:**
Test independence means **each test can run independently** without depending on other tests. Tests should have their own setup and not share state.

**Why It Matters:**

```
PROBLEMS WITH DEPENDENT TESTS:
─────────────────────────────
✗ Tests fail if run in different order
✗ One test failure breaks all following tests
✗ Can't run tests in parallel
✗ Debugging becomes nightmare
✗ Flaky and unreliable

BENEFITS OF INDEPENDENT TESTS:
───────────────────────────────
✓ Run in any order, same result
✓ Parallel execution possible
✓ Failed test doesn't cascade
✓ Easy to debug
✓ Reliable and predictable
```

**Bad Example (Dependent):**

```
static List<User> users = new ArrayList<>();  // SHARED STATE!

@Test
void testCreateUser() {
    User user = new User("John");
    users.add(user);  // Adding to shared list
    assertEquals(1, users.size());
}

@Test
void testListUsers() {
    // This test DEPENDS on testCreateUser running first!
    assertEquals(1, users.size());  // Will fail if run alone
}

Problems:
✗ testListUsers fails if testCreateUser hasn't run
✗ Tests share state
✗ Can't run in different order
✗ Can't run in parallel
```

**Good Example (Independent):**

```
@Test
void testCreateUser() {
    // Fresh setup for each test
    User user = new User("John");
    List<User> users = new ArrayList<>();  // Local, not shared
    users.add(user);
    assertEquals(1, users.size());
}

@Test
void testListUsers() {
    // Complete independent setup
    User user1 = new User("John");
    User user2 = new User("Jane");
    List<User> users = new ArrayList<>();  // New list
    users.add(user1);
    users.add(user2);
    assertEquals(2, users.size());
}

Benefits:
✓ Each test is complete
✓ Can run in any order
✓ Same result always
✓ Can run in parallel
✓ Easy to debug
```

**How to Ensure Independence:**

```
Checklist:
☑ Each test creates its own objects
☑ No shared state between tests
☑ Each test has full preconditions
☑ Test data is local, not global
☑ No test calls another test
☑ Each test resets after completion
☑ Use @Before/@Setup for common setup
☑ Use @After/@Teardown for cleanup
```

